# K3 — Basel series: Σ 1/k² for k in 1..=100M, accumulated in f64 STRICTLY IN ORDER
# (naive left-to-right; NOT Kahan-compensated, NOT reassociated). This is a serial,
# order-fixed float kernel: SIMD reassociation would change the last ulps, so NOBODY
# may vectorize it — every language here sums one term at a time, in the same order.
#
# The reduce is a dividing scalar-f64 kernel: the JIT compiles it to one native loop
# threading a poison flag for /0 (see src/jit.rs `reduce_body_divides`), and keeps the
# accumulation serial — exactly the C loop, no vectorization.
#
# Anchor = the sum at %.17f (17 decimals uniquely pins the f64, so any 1-ulp drift
# from reassociation shows up). N is hardcoded — Helix scripts have no argv, unlike
# the other languages here, which take N as argv[1]. Same convention as bench/stress.
n = read_int() ?? 100000000    # N on stdin; run.sh pipes it
print("{(1..n + 1).reduce(0.0, (a, k) => a + 1.0 / (k * k)):.17f}")
